Dear Christophe,

Thank you for asking. As the news article shows, Polaris's strong financial position means they are likely to keep placing newbuilding orders for a while. Here are my suggestions for how we should respond:

1. My main suggestion: answer Polaris's VLOC rule development proposal closely and seriously - this matters more than it looks.
In past meetings, Mr. Chong has told us, directly and indirectly, more than once: if BV cooperates and supports them well on this issue, he will keep BV as Polaris's class society for as long as he is there. This matters not just to him personally, but to the whole company - here is why. According to Mr. Chong, this case is still legally unresolved for Polaris. Their class society at the time, KR, did not cooperate, so Polaris ended up taking full blame from the Korean courts - even though, as owner, they had done proper prevention and inspection before the accident. He has repeatedly stressed how unfair this feels for Polaris. As he has clearly said, if BV, as IACS Chair, meets his expectations here even a little, we should earn Polaris's full support, across any vessel type or project.

With that in mind: on 16 September, Mr. Chong asked the major class societies to review their ideas on VLOC hull structure and damage stability, and to raise them with IACS or IMO. This is clearly Polaris's top priority right now. As you know, KPO is already discussing this with HQ, and I suggest BV sends a clear, timely answer based on that work - one good answer here will build more trust than any new sales proposal, and could be the key that truly opens a strategic partnership with Polaris. I will personally follow this up on our side.

2. I also suggest we keep the relationship with top management alive, not just at the technical level.
I will keep meetings going with Chairman Han and Mr. Do-Hoon Kim, son of the co-chairman. Right now, DH Kim doesn't feel he particularly needs to meet with class societies, so my suggestion is to find a topic and a moment where we can genuinely help him, and use that to arrange a meeting between him and you.

3. Third, I suggest we give the Qingdao Beihai VLOC project special care, since it's our best proof point with Polaris right now.
I will keep following up closely with BV China so this project runs smoothly, becomes a success story, and leaves the owner very satisfied.

4. Finally, I suggest we keep sending useful gas carrier updates, regularly and in enough depth.
Gas is a BV strength, and Polaris only wants technical meetings for a real project or a specific topic they request - so short, well-timed material works better than asking for meetings. I will handle this, with help from the Future Shipping team, to steadily build Polaris's trust in BV.

I will take the lead on all of this and ask for the meeting with Mr. Kim this week - I'll update you as it moves. Let me know if you'd like to join.

Best regards,
Sang-Jin
